      "content": "You can use Python's `requests` library to ping your API at regular intervals and log the responses. However, since you want to detect a \"remote trigger\" signal, you'll need to implement a detection mechanism that can differentiate between a scheduled command and a manual trigger.\n\nHere's a basic example of how you could structure your script:\n```python\nimport requests\nimport time\nimport hashlib\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(filename='cmd_log.log', level=logging.INFO)\n\ndef ping_api(api_url, jwt_token):\n    try:\n        response = requests.get(api_url, headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {jwt_token}'})\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        logging.info(response.json())\n    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n        logging.error(f'Error: {e}')\n\ndef detect_remote_trigger(cmd_id, previous_cmd_id, interval):\n    if cmd_id != previous_cmd_id and cmd_id != 0:\n        return True  # potential manual trigger detected\n    return False\n\ndef main():\n    api_url = 'https://yourApiUrl.com/commands'\n    jwt_token = get_jwt_token()  # replace with your authentication token\n    previous_cmd_id = 0\n    interval = 300  # ping",
